final_text:
Based on my analysis of the PCAP, I've identified a Qbot (Qakbot) infection on the victim workstation. Here is the incident report:

```json
{
  "victim": {
    "ip": "10.8.15.133",
    "mac": "00:1c:bf:03:54:82",
    "hostname": "DESKTOP-H8ALZBV",
    "user": "plucero"
  },
  "malware_family": "Qbot (Qakbot)",
  "attribution_confidence": "behavioral",
  "iocs": [
    {
      "type": "c2_endpoint",
      "value": "72.5.43.29:80",
      "evidence": "Repeated HTTP connections with spoofed IE 6 UA (Mozilla / 5.0 (compatible; MSIE 6.0; Windows NT 5.1; SV1; .NET CLR 1.0.3705)) at regular intervals. Host-only (bare-IP) HTTP header. 300+ GET and POST requests on root path / from frames 11028-18188. TCP SYN packets at regular 2-10 second intervals (epoch timestamps 1723680718-1723680857). Classic C2 beaconing behavior."
    },
    {
      "type": "domain",
      "value": "quote.checkfedexexp.com",
      "evidence": "DNS query in frame 6418. HTTP GET request from 10.8.15.133 to 104.21.55.70 (resolves to quote.checkfedexexp.com) with encoded parameters in URI: /managements?16553a25e45250a41fd5&endeds=MIGpq&JStx=59bf050d37df88a9-ade43358-eaa1220b-0571422b-0f33e6aa150e86bafd0ed4&Ld=9d7502d88d752a27b1d00587309184b5a215. Spoofed FedEx domain (legitimate is fedex.com, not checkfedexexp.com). Consistent with Qbot malspam campaign lure."
    },
    {
      "type": "domain",
      "value": "business.checkfedexexp.com",
      "evidence": "DNS query resolving malicious FedEx-themed domain. Part of same fake FedEx infrastructure."
    },
    {
      "type": "file_hash",
      "value": "ea203eb1b56387cac02308b5aca040993e2d1c7fbd06413d41c957a726e4898f",
      "evidence": "SHA256 of Windows Cabinet (.cab) archive downloaded and exported from HTTP traffic. File transferred to victim via quote.checkfedexexp.com C2 pathway. Multiple .cab files downloaded across the capture; this hash represents one of three sampled file exports."
    },
    {
      "type": "file_hash",
      "value": "e008608c81024ed297a07321b7c9ba9a00adac7cea71f24491af04b5e88f2f21",
      "evidence": "SHA256 of Windows Cabinet (.cab) archive downloaded via C2. Second sample from exported HTTP objects."
    },
    {
      "type": "file_hash",
      "value": "4839da5b25468809fbc7708652e77c4aa945e6735bf5c9119b3299653898ce34",
      "evidence": "SHA256 of Windows Cabinet (.cab) archive downloaded via C2. Third sample from exported HTTP objects."
    }
  ],
  "benign_context": [
    "Windows telemetry and update traffic (Microsoft domains, BITS transfers) to legitimate Microsoft CDN and update endpoints (msedge.b.tlu.dl.delivery.mp.microsoft.com, update.microsoft.com)",
    "Local Active Directory (lafontainebleu.org domain controller queries via LDAP/Kerberos)",
    "Standard OS connectivity (Microsoft NCSI, SmartScreen, Windows Update)"
  ],
  "incident_summary": "Workstation DESKTOP-H8ALZBV (user plucero, 10.8.15.133) is infected with Qbot malware. The infection was likely delivered via phishing email containing a malicious FedEx-themed domain (quote.checkfedexexp.com). The malware established command-and-control communication to bare-IP 72.5.43.29:80, continuously beaconing with spoofed Internet Explorer 6 HTTP headers and exfiltrating data via POST requests. Multiple Windows Cabinet archives were downloaded from the C2 server, indicating active command execution."
}
```
